\subsubsection{Sensitivity and degradation cases}
\label{sec:ou-robustness}
The robustness study is retained as a sensitivity analysis of the deployed
OU--III architecture rather than as a comparison among alternative adaptation
laws. At the nominal $H_s=\SI{1.5}{m}$ operating point, one-factor-at-a-time
(OFAT) sweeps perturb $\tau$, $\sigma_{aw}$, or $r_S$ by multipliers $0.5$,
$0.75$, $1.0$, $1.25$, and $1.5$ while holding the other two quantities fixed.
These three sweeps isolate the direct process-covariance and integral-state
regularization sensitivities and are independent of how an online adaptation
law couples the parameters.

Two additional coupled sweeps evaluate the same perturbations along the
\emph{deployed SpectralMSE manifold}. With the physical acceleration scale
perturbed by a factor $c$ at fixed $\tau$ and cadence,
\begin{equation}
 \frac{r_S(c\sigma_a,\tau)}{r_S(\sigma_a,\tau)}=c^{6/7}.
 \label{eq:robustness-sigma-coupling}
\end{equation}
For a correlation-time perturbation $\tau\mapsto c\tau$ at fixed acceleration
scale,
\begin{equation}
 \frac{r_S(c\tau)}{r_S(\tau)}
 =c^{24/7}\sqrt{\frac{T_S(\tau)}{T_S(c\tau)}}.
 \label{eq:robustness-tau-coupling}
\end{equation}
Away from the pseudo-update cadence clamps, $T_S\propto\tau$ and
Eq.~\eqref{eq:robustness-tau-coupling} reduces to
$r_S\mapsto c^{41/14}r_S$. Thus the coupled sensitivity experiment is retained,
but it now follows the analytical law actually used by the filter.

The committed robustness archive predates the analytical-coupling rewrite of
the sensitivity driver. Its three OFAT columns remain valid because the other
two tuning quantities were fixed in each sweep; its old coupled columns are not
reused as current evidence. Once the robustness bundle is regenerated by the
current driver, the publication automatically replaces the OFAT-only table by
the five-column SpectralMSE study and restores its sensitivity figure. The
low-motion and transition stress rows are separate replay cases using the
Adaptive or FixedNominal filter modes and do not rely on the retired coupled
sweep equations.

\IfFileExists{w3d-ou-robustness-sensitivity-current-generated.tex-part}{%
  \input{w3d-ou-robustness-sensitivity-current-generated.tex-part}%
}{%
  \input{w3d-ou-robustness-sensitivity-ofat-generated.tex-part}%
}

\IfFileExists{ou_robustness_sensitivity.svg}{%
  \begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \includesvg[width=\columnwidth,inkscapelatex=false]{ou_robustness_sensitivity}
    \caption{OU--III sensitivity about the nominal operating point under the
    deployed SpectralMSE regularizer. Solid curves are OFAT perturbations;
    dashed curves follow the analytical coupling in
    Eqs.~\eqref{eq:robustness-sigma-coupling}--\eqref{eq:robustness-tau-coupling}.
    Error bars are paired-bootstrap 95\% intervals and the vertical marker is
    the nominal point.}
    \label{fig:ou_robustness_sensitivity}
  \end{figure}
}{}

The OFAT results already show the local hierarchy. Over the full multiplier
range, the direct $\sigma_{aw}$ sweep is nearly flat in vertical error, the
$\tau$ sweep moves it modestly, and the $r_S$ sweep has the largest vertical
span. This does not make $\tau$ or $\sigma_{aw}$ unused: the accelerometer
observes the latent acceleration directly at \SI{200}{Hz}, so moderate prior
rescaling has less effect on vertical displacement once that channel is
information-rich. Attitude can remain more sensitive because the
latent-acceleration covariance also enters the accelerometer innovation
covariance.

A ten-seed low-motion degradation case extends the main validation envelope
without changing the deployed law. At $H_s=\SI{0.05}{m}$, the mean absolute
vertical RMS error is \SI{\OURobustnessLowStressAbsolute}{m}, corresponding to
\OURobustnessLowStressMean\% of $H_s$; the increase in normalized error exposes
the approximately fixed absolute sensor-error floor relative to the
$H_s=\SI{0.27}{m}$ reference case.

\IfFileExists{ou_robustness_stress.svg}{%
  \begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \includesvg[width=\columnwidth,inkscapelatex=false]{ou_robustness_stress}
    \caption{Ten-seed low-motion and one-way transition degradation cases. The
    Adaptive rows use the deployed OU--III configuration; the transition
    comparison includes the fixed nominal operating point as a reference.}
    \label{fig:ou_robustness_stress}
  \end{figure}
}{}

The transition stress test keeps the same low-to-high sea change but compresses
the crossfade from \SI{120}{s} to \SI{30}{s}. The Adaptive mean normalized
vertical RMS changes from \OURobustnessControlledAdaptiveMean\% to
\OURobustnessRapidAdaptiveMean\% of the final $H_s$, a paired increase of
\OURobustnessRapidDifference{} percentage points with bootstrap 95\% interval
$[\OURobustnessRapidDifferenceLow,\OURobustnessRapidDifferenceHigh]$. Even in
the rapid case, Adaptive remains lower than FixedNominal by
\OURobustnessRapidAdaptationDifference{} percentage points
($[\OURobustnessRapidAdaptationLow,\OURobustnessRapidAdaptationHigh]$).
Figure~\ref{fig:ou_robustness_stress} reports these transition bars together
with the low-motion floor.

The rapid-versus-controlled difference is a degradation result rather than a
pure adaptation-rate measurement: shortening the crossfade also changes the
composition of the fixed \SI{900}{s} scoring window. For that reason the
bidirectional deployed-SpectralMSE low--high--low protocol of
Sec.~\ref{sec:roundtrip-transition-ablation} remains the cleaner transient
adaptation diagnostic, while the one-way stress case is retained as an explicit
worst-direction robustness check.
